Well this strand of my argument goes a bit further - I argue that an 
ascension can only be made sense of in terms of the population of 
ascensions it belongs to. If you were not aiming for a particular conduct, 
your ascension belongs to the population of conduct-free ascensions and as 
such has precisely their merit, even if it happens to be one of the ones 
that don't happen to break a particular conduct.

Additionally, by being prepared to break a conduct, you're more likely to 
break it on the harder ascensions. Thus the population of ascensions which 
do keep the conduct is not precisely the same in either case, unless you 
assume random choices. Assuming random choices does not help this 
discussion, as we are talking about relative merit of ascensions with 
different attitudes to ascension, which implies differing choices. This 
decision-making process must be represented somehow in any model, if it is 
to inform the discussion.

If we assume "perfect" choices, the possible ascension paths will be 
different, as the conductless ones will diverge from the conduct ones as 
soon as the highest chance of ascension means breaking a conduct. This is 
true even for "AoLS, right from deciding whether to leve the "AoLS in your 
stash, through to being ill with 5 turns to live 7 turns from the correct 
altar, all 3 riders on your heels and an "AoLS round your neck. Only those 
ascensions where the highest-probability route to ascension means avoiding 
breaking the conduct from start to finish in every instance will have the 
same population. This case, if it exists, means that actions that break the 
conduct always lower the chances of ascension, and the conduct is 
completely meaningless.

More plausible than perfect choices is to assume choices biased towards the 
best route. In this case, all conduct ascensions will be available in both 
cases, but in the conductless case, the easier of those routes will have 
more weight (as a higher probability of choosing routes that mean breaking 
the conduct will reduce the weighting of the harder ones). Thus the 
unintentional "conduct" is devalued compared to the conduct pursued 
implacably from turn 1.

